WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:05.040
"Knock at the Cabin" invites moviegoers to&nbsp;
once again step into the twisty world of M.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:09.360
Night Shyamalan. As usual, there's more going&nbsp;
on between the credits than you might expect.

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:15.060
After the opening credits, the film cuts to a&nbsp;
young girl named Wen sitting in an open field,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.060 --> 00:00:20.700
attempting to put grasshoppers in a jar.&nbsp;
Afterwards, she uses colored pencils to write&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:25.680
cute notes and statistics about the various&nbsp;
grasshoppers, which she even assigns names.

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:30.480
Not long after, the film introduces Dave&nbsp;
Bautista's burly but sensitive character,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:35.280
Leonard, who approaches Wen. Despite some&nbsp;
initial hesitation in speaking with a stranger,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:40.860
she has a conversation with Leonard where he&nbsp;
reveals he too enjoys capturing grasshoppers. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.860 --> 00:00:45.180
urges her to be gentle, so as not to&nbsp;
spook them, foreshadowing what's to come.

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:48.900
Essentially, all four of the strangers&nbsp;
with prophetic visions will trap the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:52.260
family in their cabin and try their&nbsp;
best to keep the hostages calm as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:55.980
they consider sacrificing one of their&nbsp;
own. Obviously, this doesn't go well.

00:00:55.980 --> 00:00:59.940
These efforts at gentle enclosure&nbsp;
include attending to their medical needs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:05.160
keeping Wen safe, and giving their backstories&nbsp;
to help humanize themselves. The cabin itself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:09.780
is an allegory for the grasshopper jar — as&nbsp;
they enter, there is no chance for escape.

00:01:10.440 --> 00:01:15.780
When Leonard first appears, talking to Wen, the&nbsp;
camera begins acting unsettlingly off-balance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.780 --> 00:01:20.220
tilting further as the conversation&nbsp;
progresses. Known as a "dutch angle,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:23.880
the effect is employed to help create&nbsp;
a jarring, eerie, "off-center" feel.

00:01:23.880 --> 00:01:28.380
Director M. Night Shyamalan uses the&nbsp;
technique repeatedly to portray Leonard's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:33.300
torn psychological state. The man has a task he&nbsp;
doesn't want to do, which involves him killing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.300 --> 00:01:37.140
torturing, and carrying the burden&nbsp;
of the fate of the entire world on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:41.700
his mind. Everything in his worldview is&nbsp;
thus "off-center" and "not quite right."

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:45.840
"If you fail to choose, the world will end."

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:50.040
The use of the "dutch angle" is also in&nbsp;
keeping with Shyamalan's '90s thriller&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:54.540
theme. Classic '90s films that crafted an&nbsp;
off-kilter feel with dutch angles include&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:58.440
"12 Monkeys," "Mission Impossible,"&nbsp;
and "The City of Lost Children."

00:01:59.160 --> 00:02:03.600
After the four strangers-with-visions invade&nbsp;
the cabin and tie up Andrew and Eric while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:09.120
keeping an eye on Wen, the flat screen TV shows a&nbsp;
colorful, animated cartoon — presumably something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:13.620
Wen was watching before going to catch&nbsp;
grasshoppers earlier that day. Afterwards,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:16.680
Leonard attempts to keep things as&nbsp;
light and stress-free as possible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:21.960
saying the show looks fun and educational. He then&nbsp;
adds that, like a lot of children's entertainment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.960 --> 00:02:26.220
the show seems determined to instill in&nbsp;
kids a sense of empathy and understanding.

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:29.760
Afterwards, Leonard leads the other&nbsp;
strangers to open up about their pasts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:32.040
their jobs, their hopes and dreams.

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:35.400
"My son is going to die. His name is Charlie."

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:40.560
The point of this, as Eric later says, is to&nbsp;
give the family empathy for humanity as a whole,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:44.520
since the people sent to the cabin all&nbsp;
represented important aspects of it.

00:02:44.520 --> 00:02:49.380
Essentially, the family in the film has to make&nbsp;
the ultimate sacrifice to save the world, but that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.380 --> 00:02:53.940
sacrifice can't be made in a vacuum. They have to&nbsp;
be reminded that if they damn the world, they'll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:59.400
be essentially damning people just like themselves&nbsp;
— with families, dreams, and love in their hearts.

00:02:59.400 --> 00:03:03.300
It's easy for people to be less empathetic&nbsp;
when they are shielded or distanced from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:07.260
the suffering of others, but learning to&nbsp;
empathize with humanity — whether through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:10.740
a children's show, or through learning&nbsp;
about people's lives — is imperative.

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:15.480
News reports are frequently used as&nbsp;
exposition in many types of films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:19.740
and Shyamalan has certainly used his&nbsp;
share. He even appears in a cameo in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:23.640
"Knock at the Cabin" in an infomercial that&nbsp;
airs prior to one such Breaking News report.

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:27.660
It's a great way to dole out a lot of&nbsp;
exposition in a short amount of time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:32.280
which is great for genre films eager to&nbsp;
get backstory out of the way as quickly as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:36.900
possible and get to the more interesting&nbsp;
aspects, such as the twisty narrative.

00:03:36.900 --> 00:03:40.680
Shyamalan has a unique place in&nbsp;
this particular cinematic corner,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:45.480
however, having created one of the best&nbsp;
fake news reports in film history. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:50.640
2002's "Signs," the main family of characters&nbsp;
are stuck in their Pennsylvania home, watching&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:56.100
a news report of camcorder footage at a Brazilian&nbsp;
birthday. During a normal kids birthday party,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.100 --> 00:04:00.720
the blurry footage suddenly reveals what&nbsp;
looks to be a green-skinned, humanoid alien.

00:04:00.720 --> 00:04:05.400
The news report scenes in "Knock at the Cabin"&nbsp;
— particularly the plague of fifty-foot tidal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.400 --> 00:04:10.440
waves — is filmed in a similar way as "Signs." In&nbsp;
both films, the cameraman is filming people having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:14.580
fun, before finding themselves confronted&nbsp;
with something beyond their comprehension.

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:19.680
Are the strangers who invaded the cabin truly&nbsp;
getting horrific visions of the end times,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:24.840
or are they lying to gaslight the family into&nbsp;
harming each other, as punishment for being a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:29.160
gay couple raising an immigrant child? Are&nbsp;
these strangers living a shared delusion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.160 --> 00:04:33.960
based on a deranged echo chamber of internet&nbsp;
misinformation? Or could they be correct?

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:37.740
One early clue that the film uses&nbsp;
to substantiate the validity of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.740 --> 00:04:41.580
their visions and prophecies, as well&nbsp;
as the divine will of a vengeful God,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:46.440
is when they kill their first acolyte —&nbsp;
Rupert Grint's Redmond. After the family&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.440 --> 00:04:51.720
refuses to sacrifice one of their own, Redmond&nbsp;
gets on his knees and puts a mask over his head,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.720 --> 00:04:55.980
the other strangers getting their makeshift&nbsp;
weapons ready to ritualistically kill him.

00:04:55.980 --> 00:05:00.180
While this is happening, the viewer can see&nbsp;
how robotic and cold they get while performing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.180 --> 00:05:05.160
this sacrificial ritual, with each speaking&nbsp;
some ancient prayer in unison. Even Redmond,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.160 --> 00:05:08.160
who was initially frightened,&nbsp;
seems calm right before his death.

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:12.840
When the deed is done, the surviving&nbsp;
invaders become disoriented, perturbed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:18.120
and sick — with Leonard even vomiting in the&nbsp;
sink. This seems to indicate that once they say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:22.320
"humanity is judged," they aren't&nbsp;
in control. So who, or what, is?

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:25.740
The presence of some higher power&nbsp;
or puller-of-the-puppet-strings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.740 --> 00:05:27.660
is seemingly confirmed later in the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.660 --> 00:05:32.220
as Eric states he saw a glowing figure&nbsp;
right before Redmond was executed.

